fcntl: answer the record-lock requests instead of refusing them.

picolibc's fcntl knows F_SETLK and returns 0 for it -- there is no
advisory locking here, so nothing can conflict and "the lock was taken"
is the truthful answer. F_GETLK and F_SETLKW fell through to the
default and returned EINVAL.

That is what stopped SQLite writing. Its unixCheckReservedLock asks
F_GETLK whether another connection holds a RESERVED lock; EINVAL came
back, SQLite turned it into SQLITE_IOERR_CHECKRESERVEDLOCK, and the
sqlite3 shell printed "disk I/O error" for every CREATE TABLE and every
INSERT while SELECT on an existing database worked perfectly. Nothing
in the message pointed at locking.

The answers here are the ones a system with no record locking should
give, not convenient fictions:

  F_GETLK   fills in l_type = F_UNLCK, which means "no lock would
            conflict with the one you asked about". True: nothing on
            this machine can hold one.
  F_SETLKW  the blocking form of F_SETLK. Since the lock is always
            available it never blocks, so it is F_SETLK's answer.

If advisory locking is ever implemented in the kernel, all three become
real calls and this becomes the fallback for a kernel that lacks them.

--- a/libos/linux/fcntl.c
+++ b/libos/linux/fcntl.c
@@ -73,9 +73,31 @@
         op = LINUX_F_SETFL;
         arg = 2;                /* translate, below */
         break;
+    /*
+     * RECORD LOCKS. There is no advisory locking on this system, so no
+     * lock can ever conflict with another -- and that makes these
+     * answers correct rather than merely convenient.
+     */
     case F_SETLK:
+    case F_SETLKW:
         return 0;
+    case F_GETLK: {
+        struct flock *fl;
+
+        va_start(ap, op);
+        fl = va_arg(ap, struct flock *);
+        va_end(ap);
+        /*
+         * F_UNLCK means "nothing would stop you taking this lock".
+         * SQLite asks exactly this question before it writes, and an
+         * EINVAL here became "disk I/O error" with no mention of locks
+         * anywhere in it.
+         */
+        if (fl)
+            fl->l_type = F_UNLCK;
+        return 0;
+    }
     default:
         errno = EINVAL;
         return -1;
     }
